@page "/joins"

<div class="container-fluid">
    <div class="row mb-4">
        <div class="col">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-0">Complete Join Guide</h2>
            <p class="text-secondary small">A practical SQL Server join reference for choosing the right join, preventing duplicates, and debugging multi-table queries.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
        <div class="col-lg-8">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white h-100">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-info">
                    Join Types At A Glance
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <div class="table-responsive">
                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped align-middle mb-0">
                            <thead>
                                <tr>
                                    <th>Join</th>
                                    <th>Returns</th>
                                    <th>Use When</th>
                                </tr>
                            </thead>
                            <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>INNER JOIN</code></td>
                                    <td>Only matching rows from both sides</td>
                                    <td>You want rows only when a valid relationship exists</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>LEFT JOIN</code></td>
                                    <td>All left rows plus matching right rows</td>
                                    <td>You must keep the left table even when no match exists</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>RIGHT JOIN</code></td>
                                    <td>All right rows plus matching left rows</td>
                                    <td>Usually rewrite as <code>LEFT JOIN</code> for readability</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>FULL OUTER JOIN</code></td>
                                    <td>All rows from both sides, matched where possible</td>
                                    <td>You need to compare two sets and keep unmatched rows from both</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>CROSS JOIN</code></td>
                                    <td>Every left row paired with every right row</td>
                                    <td>You intentionally want a cartesian product</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>SELF JOIN</code></td>
                                    <td>A table joined to itself</td>
                                    <td>Hierarchies such as employee-manager or category-parent</td>
                                </tr>
                                <tr>
                                    <td><code>OUTER APPLY</code></td>
                                    <td>All left rows plus correlated result per row</td>
                                    <td>You want top 1, latest record, or row-wise lookup logic</td>
                                </tr>
                            </tbody>
                        </table>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-4">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white h-100">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-warning">
                    Fast Rules
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <div class="list-group list-group-flush">
                        <div class="list-group-item bg-transparent text-light border-secondary px-0">
                            Start from the most selective table whenever possible.
                        </div>
                        <div class="list-group-item bg-transparent text-light border-secondary px-0">
                            Join on keys, not on descriptions or formatted values.
                        </div>
                        <div class="list-group-item bg-transparent text-light border-secondary px-0">
                            Put relationship logic in <code>ON</code>; put final row filters in <code>WHERE</code>.
                        </div>
                        <div class="list-group-item bg-transparent text-light border-secondary px-0">
                            Expect row multiplication whenever one row can match many child rows.
                        </div>
                        <div class="list-group-item bg-transparent text-light border-secondary px-0">
                            Prefer <code>EXISTS</code> when you only need to know a match exists.
                        </div>
                        <div class="list-group-item bg-transparent text-light border-secondary px-0 pb-0">
                            Match data types on both sides of the join.
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white h-100">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-primary">
                    Picking The Right Base Table
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p class="text-light">The first table in the query shapes readability and often performance.</p>
                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                        <li>For reporting, start from the fact table that holds the business event.</li>
                        <li>For lookups, start from the table with the strongest filter.</li>
                        <li>For many-to-many paths, expect a bridge table in the middle.</li>
                        <li>For optional relationships, keep the required side as the left side of a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mt-3 mb-0"><code>-- Better: start from the filtered business event
SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    c.CustomerName,
    p.ProductName
FROM Orders o
INNER JOIN Customers c
    ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID
INNER JOIN OrderDetails od
    ON od.OrderID = o.OrderID
INNER JOIN Products p
    ON p.ProductID = od.ProductID
WHERE o.OrderDate &gt;= '2026-01-01'
  AND o.OrderDate &lt;  '2026-02-01';</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white h-100">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-info">
                    ON Vs WHERE
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p class="text-light">This is one of the biggest sources of accidental bugs in join queries.</p>
                    <div class="alert alert-info bg-transparent border-info">
                        <strong>Rule:</strong> filtering the joined table inside <code>WHERE</code> can turn a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> into an effective <code>INNER JOIN</code>.
                    </div>

                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>-- Keeps all customers, even when no open invoice exists
SELECT
    c.CustomerID,
    i.InvoiceID
FROM Customers c
LEFT JOIN Invoices i
    ON i.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
   AND i.Status = 'Open';</code></pre>

                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>-- This removes customers with no matching invoice
SELECT
    c.CustomerID,
    i.InvoiceID
FROM Customers c
LEFT JOIN Invoices i
    ON i.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
WHERE i.Status = 'Open';</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row mb-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-warning">
                    The Duplicate Row Problem
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p class="text-light">Most “wrong join” bugs are really cardinality bugs. If one parent row has many matching child rows, the parent row repeats once for each child match.</p>

                    <div class="row g-4">
                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-warning fw-bold">When it happens</h6>
                            <ul class="text-light">
                                <li>One customer has many orders</li>
                                <li>One order has many order lines</li>
                                <li>One employee has many history records</li>
                                <li>A many-to-many bridge table is involved</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-success fw-bold">How to fix it</h6>
                            <ul class="text-light">
                                <li>Aggregate the child rows first</li>
                                <li>Use <code>EXISTS</code> if you only need a yes/no match</li>
                                <li>Use <code>ROW_NUMBER()</code> or <code>OUTER APPLY</code> to pick one child row</li>
                                <li>Use <code>DISTINCT</code> only when duplicate suppression is truly acceptable</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>-- Pick the latest status row per order
SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    s.StatusName,
    s.StatusDate
FROM Orders o
OUTER APPLY
(
    SELECT TOP 1
        osh.StatusName,
        osh.StatusDate
    FROM OrderStatusHistory osh
    WHERE osh.OrderID = o.OrderID
    ORDER BY osh.StatusDate DESC
) s;</code></pre>

                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>-- Use EXISTS when you only need to know the child exists
SELECT
    c.CustomerID,
    c.CustomerName
FROM Customers c
WHERE EXISTS
(
    SELECT 1
    FROM Orders o
    WHERE o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
      AND o.Status = 'Open'
);</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white h-100">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold" style="color: #b388ff;">
                    Common Join Patterns
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <h6 class="fw-bold" style="color: #b388ff;">Many-to-many through a bridge</h6>
                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    s.StudentName,
    c.CourseName
FROM Students s
INNER JOIN StudentCourses sc
    ON sc.StudentID = s.StudentID
INNER JOIN Courses c
    ON c.CourseID = sc.CourseID;</code></pre>

                    <h6 class="fw-bold text-info">Anti join: rows with no match</h6>
                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>SELECT
    c.CustomerID,
    c.CustomerName
FROM Customers c
LEFT JOIN Orders o
    ON o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
WHERE o.CustomerID IS NULL;</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="col-lg-6">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white h-100">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-danger">
                    Self Join And Hierarchies
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p class="text-light">Use aliases clearly when the same table appears twice.</p>
                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    e.EmployeeName,
    m.EmployeeName AS ManagerName
FROM Employees e
LEFT JOIN Employees m
    ON m.EmployeeID = e.ManagerID;</code></pre>

                    <div class="alert alert-warning bg-transparent border-warning mb-0">
                        <strong>Tip:</strong> for deeper hierarchies, a recursive CTE is usually clearer than chaining many self joins.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row mb-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-success">
                    Performance And Troubleshooting
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <div class="row g-4">
                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-success fw-bold">Checklist</h6>
                            <ul class="text-light">
                                <li>Indexes exist on join keys and major filter columns</li>
                                <li>Data types match exactly on both sides of joins</li>
                                <li>Functions are not wrapped around indexed join/filter columns</li>
                                <li>Filters are applied as early as possible</li>
                                <li>Bridge tables are expected and handled intentionally</li>
                                <li>Estimated or actual execution plan has been reviewed</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-danger fw-bold">Symptoms And Likely Causes</h6>
                            <div class="table-responsive">
                                <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                    <thead>
                                        <tr>
                                            <th>Symptom</th>
                                            <th>Likely Cause</th>
                                        </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Too many rows</td>
                                            <td>One-to-many join or missing join predicate</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Too few rows</td>
                                            <td><code>INNER JOIN</code> where <code>LEFT JOIN</code> was needed</td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Unexpected nulls</td>
                                            <td>Optional relationship or unmatched <code>LEFT JOIN</code></td>
                                        </tr>
                                        <tr>
                                            <td>Slow query</td>
                                            <td>Bad join order, scans, implicit conversions, or missing indexes</td>
                                        </tr>
                                    </tbody>
                                </table>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mt-4 mb-0"><code>SET STATISTICS TIME ON;
SET STATISTICS IO ON;

SELECT ...
FROM ...
INNER JOIN ...
    ON ...
WHERE ...;

SET STATISTICS TIME OFF;
SET STATISTICS IO OFF;</code></pre>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row mb-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-info">
                    Reusable Join Templates
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <div class="row g-4">
                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-info fw-bold">Base inner join</h6>
                            <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>SELECT
    a.Col1,
    b.Col2
FROM TableA a
INNER JOIN TableB b
    ON b.KeyCol = a.KeyCol;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-warning fw-bold">Optional child row</h6>
                            <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>SELECT
    p.ParentName,
    c.ChildName
FROM ParentTable p
LEFT JOIN ChildTable c
    ON c.ParentID = p.ParentID;</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="fw-bold" style="color: #b388ff;">Anti join</h6>
                            <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>SELECT
    a.*
FROM TableA a
WHERE NOT EXISTS
(
    SELECT 1
    FROM TableB b
    WHERE b.KeyCol = a.KeyCol
);</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-success fw-bold">Latest child per parent</h6>
                            <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>SELECT
    p.ParentID,
    x.ChildValue
FROM ParentTable p
OUTER APPLY
(
    SELECT TOP 1 c.ChildValue
    FROM ChildHistory c
    WHERE c.ParentID = p.ParentID
    ORDER BY c.CreatedAt DESC
) x;</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="row mb-4">
        <div class="col-12">
            <div class="card glass-card text-white">
                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-warning">
                    Deep Worked Examples With Dummy Tables And Values
                </div>
                <div class="card-body">
                    <p class="text-light mb-4">
                        The examples below all use the same tiny sales database so you can see exactly why rows appear, disappear, or multiply.
                        Read them in order because each example builds on the same tables and data.
                    </p>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-warning fw-bold">1. Dummy tables</h6>
                            <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>CREATE TABLE Customers
(
    CustomerID   INT PRIMARY KEY,
    CustomerName VARCHAR(50),
    City         VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE SalesReps
(
    SalesRepID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    RepName    VARCHAR(50)
);

CREATE TABLE Orders
(
    OrderID     INT PRIMARY KEY,
    CustomerID  INT,
    SalesRepID  INT,
    OrderDate   DATE,
    Status      VARCHAR(20)
);

CREATE TABLE OrderItems
(
    OrderItemID INT PRIMARY KEY,
    OrderID     INT,
    ProductName VARCHAR(50),
    Qty         INT,
    LineAmount  DECIMAL(10,2)
);

CREATE TABLE Payments
(
    PaymentID     INT PRIMARY KEY,
    OrderID       INT,
    PaymentDate   DATE,
    Amount        DECIMAL(10,2),
    PaymentStatus VARCHAR(20)
);</code></pre>
                        </div>

                        <div class="col-lg-6">
                            <h6 class="text-info fw-bold">2. Dummy values</h6>
                            <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary mb-0"><code>INSERT INTO Customers VALUES
(1, 'Alice', 'Delhi'),
(2, 'Bob',   'Mumbai'),
(3, 'Carol', 'Bengaluru');

INSERT INTO SalesReps VALUES
(10, 'Nina'),
(11, 'Ravi');

INSERT INTO Orders VALUES
(1001, 1, 10, '2026-01-10', 'Open'),
(1002, 1, 11, '2026-01-15', 'Closed'),
(1003, 2, 10, '2026-01-20', 'Open');

INSERT INTO OrderItems VALUES
(1, 1001, 'Laptop',   1, 80000.00),
(2, 1001, 'Mouse',    2,  1000.00),
(3, 1002, 'Keyboard', 1,  2500.00),
(4, 1003, 'Monitor',  2, 24000.00);

INSERT INTO Payments VALUES
(501, 1001, '2026-01-11', 40000.00, 'Captured'),
(502, 1001, '2026-01-12', 40000.00, 'Captured'),
(503, 1002, '2026-01-16',  2500.00, 'Captured');</code></pre>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="alert alert-info bg-transparent border-info mb-4">
                        <strong>Keep this data picture in mind:</strong>
                        Alice has two orders, Bob has one order, Carol has no order.
                        Order <code>1001</code> has two items and two payments.
                        Order <code>1003</code> has one item but no payment.
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-primary">
                                    Example 1: INNER JOIN keeps only matching rows
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    c.CustomerName,
    o.OrderID,
    o.Status
FROM Customers c
INNER JOIN Orders o
    ON o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
ORDER BY c.CustomerName, o.OrderID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>CustomerName</th>
                                                    <th>OrderID</th>
                                                    <th>Status</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>Alice</td><td>1001</td><td>Open</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>Alice</td><td>1002</td><td>Closed</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>Bob</td><td>1003</td><td>Open</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>What happened:</strong> Carol does not appear because <code>INNER JOIN</code> returns only rows that match on both sides.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>Alice appears twice because she has two matching orders.</li>
                                        <li>Bob appears once because he has one matching order.</li>
                                        <li>Carol disappears because there is no order row with <code>CustomerID = 3</code>.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-info">
                                    Example 2: LEFT JOIN keeps the left side even when no match exists
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    c.CustomerName,
    o.OrderID,
    o.Status
FROM Customers c
LEFT JOIN Orders o
    ON o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
ORDER BY c.CustomerName, o.OrderID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>CustomerName</th>
                                                    <th>OrderID</th>
                                                    <th>Status</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>Alice</td><td>1001</td><td>Open</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>Alice</td><td>1002</td><td>Closed</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>Bob</td><td>1003</td><td>Open</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>Carol</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>What changed:</strong> Carol now stays in the result because the left table is <code>Customers</code>.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>The left table decides which rows are guaranteed to remain.</li>
                                        <li>Missing columns from the right side become <code>NULL</code>.</li>
                                        <li>This pattern is ideal for "show all master rows, even if no activity exists".</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-danger">
                                    Example 3: One-to-many joins multiply rows
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    oi.ProductName,
    oi.Qty,
    oi.LineAmount
FROM Orders o
INNER JOIN OrderItems oi
    ON oi.OrderID = o.OrderID
ORDER BY o.OrderID, oi.OrderItemID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>OrderID</th>
                                                    <th>ProductName</th>
                                                    <th>Qty</th>
                                                    <th>LineAmount</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>1001</td><td>Laptop</td><td>1</td><td>80000.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1001</td><td>Mouse</td><td>2</td><td>1000.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1002</td><td>Keyboard</td><td>1</td><td>2500.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1003</td><td>Monitor</td><td>2</td><td>24000.00</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>Why order 1001 appears twice:</strong> one order matches two order-item rows.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>The query is not wrong. It is faithfully returning each matching pair.</li>
                                        <li>If you expected one row per order, this result is too granular.</li>
                                        <li>Whenever the child table stores detail rows, the parent repeats once per child row.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-success">
                                    Example 4: Aggregate the child table first to get one row per parent
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    x.ItemCount,
    x.OrderTotal
FROM Orders o
LEFT JOIN
(
    SELECT
        oi.OrderID,
        COUNT(*) AS ItemCount,
        SUM(oi.LineAmount) AS OrderTotal
    FROM OrderItems oi
    GROUP BY oi.OrderID
) x
    ON x.OrderID = o.OrderID
ORDER BY o.OrderID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>OrderID</th>
                                                    <th>ItemCount</th>
                                                    <th>OrderTotal</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>1001</td><td>2</td><td>81000.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1002</td><td>1</td><td>2500.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1003</td><td>1</td><td>24000.00</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>Why this works:</strong> the subquery reduces <code>OrderItems</code> to one row per order before the join happens.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>The outer query now joins order-to-order instead of order-to-order-item.</li>
                                        <li>This is the cleanest fix when you need summary data from a detail table.</li>
                                        <li>Think "shape the child rows first, then join".</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold" style="color: #b388ff;">
                                    Example 5: EXISTS is often better than joining when you only need yes or no
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    c.CustomerID,
    c.CustomerName
FROM Customers c
WHERE EXISTS
(
    SELECT 1
    FROM Orders o
    WHERE o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
      AND o.Status = 'Open'
)
ORDER BY c.CustomerID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>CustomerID</th>
                                                    <th>CustomerName</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>1</td><td>Alice</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>2</td><td>Bob</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>Why not just join Orders?</strong> because a join could repeat the customer if multiple open orders exist.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li><code>EXISTS</code> stops caring after the first qualifying match.</li>
                                        <li>It expresses intent clearly: "show customers for whom at least one row exists".</li>
                                        <li>This is a semi-join pattern and is often both cleaner and safer.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-primary">
                                    Example 6: NOT EXISTS finds rows that do not have a match
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    c.CustomerID,
    c.CustomerName
FROM Customers c
WHERE NOT EXISTS
(
    SELECT 1
    FROM Orders o
    WHERE o.CustomerID = c.CustomerID
)
ORDER BY c.CustomerID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>CustomerID</th>
                                                    <th>CustomerName</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>3</td><td>Carol</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>Use case:</strong> finding idle customers, products never sold, users with no login, or employees with no assignment.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>This is usually safer than <code>NOT IN</code>, especially when nulls can appear.</li>
                                        <li>It answers "which parent rows are completely unmatched?"</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4 mb-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-info">
                                    Example 7: OUTER APPLY gets the latest related row per parent
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    p.PaymentDate,
    p.Amount
FROM Orders o
OUTER APPLY
(
    SELECT TOP 1
        py.PaymentDate,
        py.Amount
    FROM Payments py
    WHERE py.OrderID = o.OrderID
    ORDER BY py.PaymentDate DESC
) p
ORDER BY o.OrderID;</code></pre>

                                    <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                                        <table class="table table-dark table-striped mb-0">
                                            <thead>
                                                <tr>
                                                    <th>OrderID</th>
                                                    <th>PaymentDate</th>
                                                    <th>Amount</th>
                                                </tr>
                                            </thead>
                                            <tbody>
                                                <tr><td>1001</td><td>2026-01-12</td><td>40000.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1002</td><td>2026-01-16</td><td>2500.00</td></tr>
                                                <tr><td>1003</td><td>NULL</td><td>NULL</td></tr>
                                            </tbody>
                                        </table>
                                    </div>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>Why this is useful:</strong> you want only one payment row per order, usually the latest, earliest, or highest one.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>A normal join would return two payment rows for order <code>1001</code>.</li>
                                        <li><code>OUTER APPLY</code> lets you run a small correlated subquery per parent row.</li>
                                        <li>It is very handy for "top 1 child row" scenarios.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="row g-4">
                        <div class="col-12">
                            <div class="card bg-dark border-secondary">
                                <div class="card-header border-secondary fw-bold text-danger">
                                    Example 8: The classic LEFT JOIN mistake with WHERE
                                </div>
                                <div class="card-body">
                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>-- Correct: keep all orders, only attach captured payments
SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    p.PaymentStatus
FROM Orders o
LEFT JOIN Payments p
    ON p.OrderID = o.OrderID
   AND p.PaymentStatus = 'Captured';</code></pre>

                                    <pre class="bg-black text-success p-3 rounded border border-secondary"><code>-- Wrong for this requirement: order 1003 disappears
SELECT
    o.OrderID,
    p.PaymentStatus
FROM Orders o
LEFT JOIN Payments p
    ON p.OrderID = o.OrderID
WHERE p.PaymentStatus = 'Captured';</code></pre>

                                    <p class="text-light mb-2"><strong>Why the second query is dangerous:</strong> <code>WHERE p.PaymentStatus = 'Captured'</code> rejects the rows where payment columns are null, so unpaid orders vanish.</p>
                                    <ul class="text-light mb-0">
                                        <li>If your business rule is "keep every order", the filter belongs in <code>ON</code>.</li>
                                        <li>If your business rule is "show only orders that have captured payments", then the <code>WHERE</code> version may be correct.</li>
                                        <li>The right answer depends on intent, not just syntax.</li>
                                    </ul>
                                </div>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="alert alert-warning bg-transparent border-warning mt-4 mb-0">
                        <strong>Final mental model:</strong>
                        every join takes one row from the left side and tries to pair it with zero, one, or many rows from the right side.
                        Once you ask "how many matches can this row have?", most join bugs become much easier to reason about.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

